Step 1: Frequency response [latex] H(j\omega) =
\begin{cases}
1, & |\omega| \leq 200\pi \
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} [/latex] Step 2: Find impulse response [latex] h(t) [/latex] by inverse Fourier transform [latex] h(t) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} H(j\omega) e^{j\omega t} d\omega [/latex] But since [latex] H(j\omega) = 1 [/latex] for [latex] |\omega| \leq 200\pi [/latex], and 0 otherwise: [latex] h(t) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_{-200\pi}^{200\pi} e^{j\omega t} d\omega [/latex] Step 3: Evaluate the integral This is a standard Fourier inverse transform result: [latex] h(t) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \cdot \frac{2 \sin(200\pi t)}{t} = \frac{200 \sin(200\pi t)}{\pi t} [/latex] This is the sinc function scaled: [latex] h(t) = 200 \cdot \text{sinc}(200 t) [/latex] where [latex] \text{sinc}(x) = \frac{\sin(\pi x)}{\pi x} [/latex] Step 4: Find [latex] h(0) [/latex] Use the standard limit: [latex] \lim_{t \to 0} \frac{\sin(200\pi t)}{\pi t} = 200 [/latex] So: [latex] h(0) = \frac{200 \sin(0)}{\pi \cdot 0} = 200 [/latex] Final Answer: 200

